00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:17.124
them down into just 15 minutes and you can
go to blinkist.com/microcosmos to learn more.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:34.280
There are plenty of ways to get food in the
microcosmos, but the cilia-driven vortex seems

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.750
particularly charming.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:42.440
The microbes who use this method can graze
in place if they want to, stirring up whirls

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.920
and whirls and whirls of water to draw food
in towards them.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.850
Rotifers, like this philodina here, use the
crown of cilia around their head to create

00:00:53.850 --> 00:01:00.309
that movement in the water and cycle food
like algae into closer proximity.

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:06.530
But watch carefully: if you tap the slide
and momentarily shock the rotifer into curling

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.640
inward, it’ll stop juggling its algae meal.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:17.610
And more strikingly, the algae itself immediately
stops moving.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.369
Any additional movement you see in the algae
is because the rotifer’s movements are displacing

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:23.490
the water.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.000
When the rotifer’s cilia were in motion,
the algae looked like they were caught up

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.000
in a fast-moving whirlpool.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.170
Now, it’s almost like they’re stuck in
molasses.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.650
And in a way, they kind of are.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.290
It is not that the algae are actually in molasses.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:45.659
Like all our other samples, they’re living
it up in good old H20.

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:50.270
And of course to us, liquid water is a flowing
thing.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:56.420
We can swim through it, jump into it, run
our hands through it, gather samples for this

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.600
show from it--all with relative ease.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.750
But for microbes, water isn’t like that.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.060
It’s like a syrup.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:13.080
And while life in syrup sounds whimsical,
the mechanics and math that explains it is

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.040
much less so.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:22.319
And thus we are going to give you just a very
broad idea of what’s going on here.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:30.250
Warning, fluid dynamics ahead.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:36.200
The way things move in a fluid is an extremely
complex, dynamic thing that’s characterized

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.540
and defined in many different ways.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.370
But the most useful description for us is
what’s called the Reynolds number, which

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:50.640
tells us whether or not the movement we’re
interested in is dominated by inertia or viscosity.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.730
When the Reynolds number is higher, the inertial
forces dominate, which means that an object

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.209
or fluid that is gliding is more likely to
keep on gliding.

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:06.260
And when the Reynolds number is lower, the
viscous forces dominate, and that brings things

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.202
to a halt.

00:03:12.262 --> 00:03:17.170
If we’re comparing things moving in water,
then one of the most useful things to note

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:22.159
is that an organism’s Reynolds number will
generally increase with its size.

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:27.819
A person swimming in water might have a Reynolds
number that’s on the order of 10,000, which

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:30.320
lets us glide a little between each stroke.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.409
A blue whale  might be somewhere
on the order of 100 million, which lets it

00:03:35.409 --> 00:03:39.959
travel quite far with just a flap of its tail.

00:03:39.959 --> 00:03:44.670
Bacteria, by comparison, they’re working
with a Reynolds number that’s more on the

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:49.739
order of 0.00001.

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:55.120
And where a whale or a person can travel multiple
body lengths depending on how we generate

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:01.409
power, a bacteria that’s been pushed by
something else will only travel about a tenth

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:07.150
of the diameter of a hydrogen atom before
coming to a stop.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.090
That is not far.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:16.530
So for the microcosmos, water is not the lovely
flowing thing that we know and love.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:22.370
It’s thick and syrupy, and it should be
a challenge to move through.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:27.380
But as you may have noticed, many of these
microbes are still plenty fast.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.710
Sometimes we get questions about whether we’ve
sped up the footage, and the answer is that

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:37.930
unless we’ve otherwise noted any changes
in speed, that is the real-time speed of these

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.340
organisms.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:45.280
Even in this clip, the various flagellates
and ciliates are moving quite fast--and that’s

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.471
after we slowed down the footage 500%.

00:04:49.562 --> 00:04:52.372
So like…how?

00:04:52.372 --> 00:04:56.330
How can they move so fast in syrup?

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:01.900
We went into more detail about how single-celled
eukaryotes do this many episodes ago.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.090
Some use cilia, others use flagella.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:09.710
And while these are different structures with
their own motions, the effect is the same.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.270
Cilia and flagella generate thrust, which
keeps the organism moving.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:21.926
So protists have evolved these various appendages
that allow them to move in a viscous environment.

00:05:21.926 --> 00:05:28.139
And that’s because movement is essential,
whether it’s within a confined area or freely

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:29.390
through space.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:48.320
It takes hunters to their prey, and it takes
prey away from their hunters.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:54.591
So for microbes, the syrupy world around them
is actually quite important, and it’s one

00:05:54.591 --> 00:05:57.650
scientists are working to understand better.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:03.389
They’ve studied how these didinium chase
their prey in changing temperatures and viscosity.

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:08.680
They’ve found that increasing the viscosity
of a rotifer’s surroundings slowed its population

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:09.680
growth.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.970
And they’ve tested how viscosity affects
bacterial movement too.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.470
These responses are important for understanding
not only the individual lives of microbes

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.040
and the ecosystems they live in.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:27.180
They’re important for understanding our
own bodies because to many microbes, we are

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.699
just another strange geographic feature to
live in.

00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:38.840
We’re a living bag of fluids that bacteria
often find their way into.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:43.083
And how they navigate our body deeply impacts
our health.

00:06:47.723 --> 00:06:52.030
Spirochetes, for example, cause Lyme disease
and syphilis.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:57.770
But to do that, they have to get through blood
and other fluids, which they do in this erratic,

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.220
corkscrew motion.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:04.660
The bacteria is generating thrust with flagella
(though it’s important to note that bacterial

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:10.759
flagella are a completely distinct thing from
the eukaryotic flagella we mentioned earlier).

00:07:10.759 --> 00:07:16.280
And spirochetes use their flagella in a way
that’s weird even for bacteria.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:22.560
They keep their flagella inside them, sandwiched
between their membranes.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:29.830
And as the flagella rotate internally, they
drive the entire spirochete to rotate and move.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:36.880
This mechanism doesn’t just move the spirochete,
it also helps them dig deeper into the dense

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:42.720
bits inside us that are more difficult for
other bacteria to penetrate.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.750
And that’s just one example of how a life
in syrup impacts us.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:53.720
Sometimes the syrup is water, and sometimes
it’s you.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.800
But for microbes, it’s just home.

